5BSD runs Linux binaries natively — and every Linux syscall passes
through the MAC framework before it executes. See mac_abac(4) and the
5BSD Epic for the security stack Linux programs can't see.
%
Want a file descriptor that only works in the process it was minted
for? vnode claims and capability transfer states (CAP_XFER_ONCE,
CAP_XFER_NONE) make a leaked fd useless. See the Capability Transfer
chapter of the 5BSD Epic.
%
capprotect can make a process invisible to ps(1), immune to ptrace(2),
and immune to kill(2) — enforced by the MAC framework, controlled by a
capability fd.
%
Group processes, jails, and sockets into a coalition and close one fd
to tear them all down — with grace periods, deadlines, and watchdogs.
See the Process Protections chapter of the 5BSD Epic.
%
TrustedZFS hands out ZFS datasets as capability file descriptors: the
fd is the authority. Request one with "tzfsctl request", and see what
the broker offers with "tzfsctl list-flavors".
%
Need a scratch filesystem that disappears on close? Try an ephemeral
TrustedZFS handle: tzfsctl(8) can mint anonymous datasets backed by
the pool configured in tzfs.conf(5).
%
5BSD boots through authority-init and serviced before running the classic
rc(8) scripts. "authorityctl status" shows the capability broker;
"servicectl services" shows managed services.
%
Services on 5BSD declare their needs in a UCL manifest inside a .cap
bundle under /Capabilities/System — the kernel grants the declared
authority before exec. See serviced(5).
%
WASPNest is 5BSD's hypervisor (the bhyve lineage). Guests get modern
VirtIO devices — fs, gpu, balloon, iommu, rtc, vsock — plus packed
rings and multiqueue.
%
AF_VSOCK sockets work between a WASPNest host and its guests with no
network configuration at all: connect to a guest by context ID.
%
Trace every branch a program executes with bsdtrace(8) and Intel
Processor Trace — then decode it offline into call trees and flame
graphs. Requires an Intel CPU; the hwt(4) framework also supports ARM
SPE on arm64.
%
bsdinstruments ships over 200 DTrace-powered profiles for one-command
system investigation, and libotelexport streams the results as
OpenTelemetry.
%
The blued(8) Bluetooth daemon owns the radio; meshd(8) provisions and
configures BLE mesh networks through it. Try "meshctl cfg" to drive
the mesh Config Client.
%
Ports and packages from FreeBSD generally just work: 5BSD tracks
FreeBSD 16-CURRENT underneath. Where 5BSD has diverged, the 5BSD Epic
— not the FreeBSD Handbook — is the source of truth.
%
The VBSD kernel is GENERIC plus an identity: every 5BSD kernel option
lives in GENERIC itself, so custom kernel configs inherit the full
feature set with a one-line include.
%
Wondering what a daemon is? 5BSD system daemons tag their log lines —
[AUTHORITY], [SERVICE], [TZFS] — so a single grep separates the init
broker, service manager, and storage broker.
%
Pass a secret to a child without an environment variable leak: an
envfd(2) descriptor holds one write-once value, can be sealed to
capability mode, and zeroes its memory on close.
%
Watch every file open, exec, and mount on the system in real time:
OpenEndpointSecurity delivers kernel security events to subscribed
clients. Try oeslogger(8).
%
mac_abac(4) gives you attribute-based access control: label files and
processes with key=value pairs via extended attributes, then write
ordered allow/deny/transition rules — no recompiling, no reboots.
%
Capsicum capability mode works on Linux binaries under 5BSD: cap_enter
a Linux process and it can no longer open new files, sockets, or
devices — only use the descriptors it already holds.
%
A process descriptor beats a PID: procdesc fds cannot be reused by a
recycled PID, work in capability mode, and can be enlisted in a
coalition so the child dies when you close the fd.
%
mac_capability services expose DTrace probes for every capability
grant and denial. "dtrace -l | grep mac_capability" lists them; the
canned scripts live in /usr/share/dtrace.
%
Measure CPU package power, temperature, and frequency over time with
hwtlm(8) — hardware telemetry from RAPL and sensors, exportable as
OpenTelemetry.
%
No Bluetooth hardware? The ng_hci_virt netgraph node emulates a
controller, so blued(8), meshd(8), and the whole test suite run in a
VM or CI without a USB radio.
%
Run Rocky Linux userlands on 5BSD storage: "tzfsctl request -f linux"
materializes a glibc rootfs from the tzfs-flavors catalog as a
capability-controlled ZFS dataset.
%
"servicectl services" shows every managed service, its state, and
restart policy; "servicectl status" summarizes the manager itself.
%
The waspnest(8) command is bhyve(8): the hypervisor is being renamed
as part of 5BSD's WASPNest virtualization stack, and both names work
today.
%
Read the whole story: the 5BSD Epic covers the security stack,
WASPNest, TrustedZFS, Bluetooth, and operations, chapter by chapter.
It lives in docs/book in the source tree.
%
Everything in the VBSD kernel is stock GENERIC plus identity — diff
sys/amd64/conf/VBSD against GENERIC and see for yourself.
%
